{
  "requirement": "`ToolUsage` holds the cumulative usage for one tool.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct ToolUsage {\n    pub calls: u64,\n    pub result_bytes: u64,\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for ToolUsage;\nimpl Copy for ToolUsage;\nimpl Debug for ToolUsage;\nimpl Default for ToolUsage;\nimpl Serialize for ToolUsage;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage` is a pure API-shape requirement. It specifies a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with public `calls: u64` and `result_bytes: u64` fields. It also specifies the trait impls `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Default` and `Serialize`. It has no behavioral clauses of its own. The `.calls`, `.result_bytes`, `.by_name` and `.replay` sub-requirements are refinements with their own tests, so I did not assess them here.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` covers the shape obligations:\n- It generates rustdoc for the SDK. `documented_exports` walks every exported struct page and calls `check_declaration` against the requirement text found through the inventory's declaration link.\n- `check_declaration` extracts the rust,ignore declaration block and compares it to the rustdoc item declaration through `signature_parts`. Both sets must match exactly, so the following would be caught:\n  - a changed or missing field;\n  - a changed field type;\n  - a changed field name;\n  - a changed visibility;\n  - a missing `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- `check_declaration` also asserts that a struct with public fields carries `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- `check_declaration` compares the trait-implementation block from the requirement with `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)` by set equality. A missing or extra `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Default` or `Serialize` impl would fail the test. `Serialize` is a required trait and is covered by this comparison.\n- The test also checks that the struct is in the inventory at the right path. Missing or unexpected exports fail.\n\nThe requirement states no serialized format and no `Default` value beyond the trait. The tests therefore need no extra assertions on those. I found no gap.",
  "references": "0d76678f3ab137bf77b6a76d0cd17c460f60426827a412971b8cd589c65e25da",
